what abt this

Your diagram is clean, well-formatted, and logically solid, but it needs a few critical adjustments to satisfy the formal attack tree format and your assessment rubric:

1. Critical Fix: Add Logic Gates (OR / AND)
In formal cybersecurity attack trees, parent nodes connect to child nodes via logic gates:

Between Root and Level 2: Add an AND or sequential gate, because to succeed, an attacker must Inject instructions AND Cause processing AND Extract/Expose the key.

Between Level 2 and Level 3 (Leaf nodes):

Under Inject Malicious Instructions: Add an OR gate (an attacker needs either RAG Document or User Prompt or Indirect Injection).

Under Extract and Expose Key: Add an OR gate (either Model Response or Attacker Destination or System Logs).

Quick way to add in draw.io: Drag a small diamond or circle shape from the left sidebar labeled OR or AND onto the connector lines between the levels.

2. Add the Required Scoring & MITRE Box
According to your task rubric (Task 3 / Screenshot 3), you need explicit scoring visible on the canvas. Add a text container in an open corner (e.g., bottom-right):

Likelihood (L): 4

Impact (I): 5

Risk Score: L×I=20 (Critical)

MITRE ATLAS Mapping: AML.T0054 (LLM Jailbreak) / AML.T0024 (Exfiltration via ML Artifacts)

Summary Verdict
Once you add the small OR / AND indicator shapes and the Risk/ATLAS metadata box, this diagram is 100% submission-ready.

